The parameters of the Nassar observer and of M_ZIKA were related to the general questionnaire factor and to the five focal questionnaires with the RQ3 models of tambi_rq3_models.R, run through tambi_rq3_alt_models.R (see 

RQ3 for the models). The outcome is the posterior mean of the parameter, standardised within the sample. Per parameter and score there are two linear models, y_z ~ cond + q_z (across-condition slope) and y_z ~ cond * q_z (score term and its condition dependence), with sum-to-zero planned contrasts for the instruction condition. Sensitivity analyses are tests with HC3 covariance and the plausible-values analysis over 100 posterior draws per participant (Rubin’s rules). The sample is the 95 participants with questionnaire scores (None 37, Partial 36, Full 22). Slopes are in parameter SD per score SD.

General factor. No parameter of either model has an across-condition slope on the general factor (Nassar observer: all p ≥ .33; M_ZIKA: all p ≥ .13, largest precision_weighting −0.16 [−0.36, 0.05]). The condition dependence of the threshold slope of M_ZIKA has p = .036. The slope is −0.30 [−0.61, 0.01] in None (p = .060), +0.28 in Partial (p = .13) and +0.18 in Full (p = .34); Partial − None 0.58 [0.10, 1.06], p = .018.

Five scales. No across-condition slope of either model has p < .10. The condition dependence of the threshold slope of M_ZIKA follows the general factor for BDI-II (p = .021; None −0.36, p = .018) and STICSA trait (p = .024; None −0.31, p = .050). The surprise_rate slope of the Nassar observer depends on the condition for BDI-II (p = .045; Partial −0.46, p = .024).

Number of latent states. From the replay of each participant with the posterior draws (replay_trial.csv; mean over draws per trial), six measures per participant were computed and related to the scores (tambi_nstate_scores.py; y (z) ~ score (z) + condition, OLS with HC3; BH over the six measures, separately for each score):

the number of states at the end of the session;

the mean number of states over the session;

the number of states at the end of the first half;

the expected number of new states in the whole session and in the second half;

the expected number of switches between states.

The model holds two states at the end of the session for most participants: median 2.00, interquartile range 2.00 to 2.02, range 1.14 to 4.58. Eleven participants hold more than 2.1 states (eight in None, three in Partial, none in Full), and four hold fewer than 1.9. New states are created almost only in the first half (second half: mean 0.01). None of the measures relates to the general factor:

states at the end of the session +0.08 SD per SD [−0.19, 0.34], p = .56;

switches +0.05, p = .76;

all six measures q ≥ .76;

Spearman on the condition residuals |ρ| ≤ .07.

The same holds for STICSA trait (all q ≥ .65), within the conditions (all slopes p ≥ .07) and for holding more than 2.1 states (logistic, general factor +0.31 log-odds, p = .32). Higher scorers therefore do not represent more latent states in the n-state model. The same holds in the hybrid model, where the type of structure, two coupled or four independent states, is unrelated to the general factor (

Hybrid Switch Model). The small spread of the number of states (81 of 96 participants between 1.9 and 2.1) limits the sensitivity of this test.

The threshold lies on the shared dimension described above. In None, higher scores go with a lower threshold, that is, with surprising attacks changing the belief about the current state more readily; in Partial and Full the slope points the other way, without p < .05 in either condition.

For each model, a reliability diagram relates the classifier’s predicted probability for that model (one versus rest) to the empirical frequency with which that model generated the data. The Expected Calibration Error (ECE) quantifies the deviation from perfect calibration:\text{ECE} = \sum_{b=1}^{B} \frac{n_b}{N} \left| \text{acc}(b) - \text{conf}(b) \right|

where the confidence range [0, 1] is partitioned into B = 10 bins, n_b is the number of samples in bin b, and \text{acc}(b) and \text{conf}(b) are the mean accuracy and confidence within that bin.

