All values lie far below the target of 0.05. Because almost all predicted probabilities are close to 0 or 1, nearly all simulations fall into the two outermost bins, and the ECE is informative about calibration only at those extremes. It does not test the calibration of intermediate probabilities, which rarely occur in the simulations.



Reliability diagrams per model.

For each state z \in \{0, 1\}, the observation likelihood under the regular and oddball components is:L_{\text{reg}}^{(z)} = f_{\text{vM}}(\omega_t; \hat{\mu}_{c_t, z}, \kappa_{\text{fixed}}) + \epsilonL_{\text{odd}}^{(z)} = f_{\text{vM}}(\omega_t; \hat{\mu}_{c_t, z} + \hat{\Delta}, \kappa_{\text{fixed}}) + \epsilon

where \epsilon = 10^{-300} prevents numerical underflow. The marginal likelihood per state is:L^{(z)} = (1 - \rho_{\text{eff}}) \cdot L_{\text{reg}}^{(z)} + \rho_{\text{eff}} \cdot L_{\text{odd}}^{(z)}

Note: The likelihoods use \kappa_{\text{fixed}} (not \kappa_{\text{eff}}), while the predictive distribution uses \kappa_{\text{eff}} = \kappa_{\text{fixed}} \cdot \text{pw}. This prevents confoundings and interaction effects as well as an over-weighting of the scalar (by scaling two entities in a single belief update / prediction loop).

The raw Bayesian posterior over the latent state is:p_t^{\text{Bayes}}(z = 1) = \frac{p_t^{\text{pred}}(z = 1) \cdot L^{(1)}}{p_t^{\text{pred}}(z = 0) \cdot L^{(0)} + p_t^{\text{pred}}(z = 1) \cdot L^{(1)}}

The momentum-dampened update is:p_t(z = 1) = (1 - \text{bm}) \cdot p_t^{\text{Bayes}}(z = 1) + \text{bm} \cdot p_{t-1}(z = 1)

with clipping to [10^{-6}, 1 - 10^{-6}] for numerical stability.

This chapter covers the blinks recorded during the task:

blinks time-locked to the cue, the attack and the feedback, with the post-feedback blink after a bite as the candidate defensive reflex;

the blink rate per trial phase and over the session, and its relation to the uncertainty and surprise signals of the latent-state model (Nexus);

the instruction condition, the general factor and STICSA trait;

whether blinks could account for the pupil results of 

Pupil Analysis.

Scripts (analysis repository): tambi_blinks_data.py (pipeline step blinkdata) and tambi_blinks_analysis.py (blinks); reproducing step 8.23. Output: stats_results/23_11/blinks/.

This chapter relates the pupil responses of each trial to:

the quantities of the latent-state model (state uncertainty before the outcome, surprise of the outcome, belief change after it);

the participant’s updating of the net placement;

transitions and oddballs;

bites and blocked attacks when a net was on the attack slot;

the instruction condition and the questionnaire scores.

The pupil signal is the cleaned and normalised signal of 

Pupil Preprocessing.

Scripts (analysis repository): tambi_pupil_model_replay.py (pipeline step pupilreplay), tambi_pupil_analysis_data.py (pupildata), tambi_pupil_models.R (pupilmodels), tambi_pupil_pause_check.R (pupilpause), tambi_pupil_scores_condition.R (pupilcond), tambi_pupil_participant.py (pupilpart), tambi_pupil_corrections.py (pupilcorr), tambi_pupil_figures.py (pupilfigs); reproducing step 8.13. Output: stats_results/pupil_analysis/.

Of the 90 participants with pupil data, 85 are analysed (None 32, Partial 33, Full 20; pupil_analysis_participants.csv). Not analysed:

15265 (excluded participant);

16381, 15289 and 13855 (fewer than 50 % usable pupil trials);

5272 (fewer than 50 usable trials, truncated file).

The model quantities come from the parameter posteriors of run 23_11. The questionnaire models use the 84 analysed participants with all five questionnaires and the general factor.

Trials after a pause. A state rating screen comes before the first trial and after trial 7 or 14 of an episode. A break screen comes at a few further points. Rating screens never fall between two episodes. The cue baseline of the first trial after such a screen is much larger than that of the other trials: after a rating screen +1.06 z (SE .03) and after a break +1.89 z (SE .06), relative to trials that directly follow the previous trial (model C_pause_baseline). The 1,097 trials after a rating or break screen (after_pause in pupil_analysis_trials.csv) are left out of all models and figures. 14,183 trials remain, 12,796 of them with an attack.

tambi_pupil_models.R fits linear mixed models (lme4, REML, Satterthwaite df).

Predictors: a model quantity x that varies within participants enters as the trial deviation from the participant mean (x_w, in SD of the pooled within-participant deviations) and as the participant mean (x_b, z-scored across participants).

Covariates: trial number, condition (contrasts Full vs None + Partial and Partial vs None), and blocked for the attack and feedback windows.

Random effects: participant intercept and slope of x_w; the slope is dropped when the fit is singular (column re).

By-condition slopes: from the same model with the terms nested in condition, with pairwise differences (lmerTest::contest).

Residual diagnostics: diagnostics/model_diagnostics.csv. The attack-window models have heavy-tailed residuals (excess kurtosis about 6), as have the bite-vs-blocked models of the attack pupil, the belief update and the surprise (6 to 11); the others are at or below 3.

Correction. Benjamini-Hochberg, with all families defined in tambi_pupil_corrections.py. A family is the set of tests that answer one question about one measure:

the couplings of one pupil window with the model quantities (attack window: surprise, belief update, prediction error, oddball attribution; feedback: surprise, belief update; anticipation and placement: state and spatial uncertainty; ISI and baseline: state uncertainty alone);

the event contrasts of one window in one condition;

the bite effects on the two pupil windows, on the two updating measures and on the two model quantities;

the slopes of one coupling in the three conditions, and separately the pairwise differences between the conditions;

for the questionnaires, the same groupings per score; the score level across the six windows.

All 90 participants with gaze data are processed (None 35, Partial 35, Full 20; 89 included in the analyses, 15265 is excluded). The left eye was recorded in 85 of them, the right eye in 5. Participants 11872 and 5272 contribute the trials of their truncated files (163 and 13 trials). EyeLink pupil size is a diameter in arbitrary units; the participant median ranges from 4,073 to 8,926 units.

The pupil stream consists of 20 ms bins: the mean of the valid 1000 Hz samples in the bin and the share of valid samples (valid = pupil size above 0 and gaze on record; 

Conversion). Each trial is one recording block, which starts about 200 ms before the colour cue and ends at the end of the outcome phase.

Script: tambi_power_simulation.py (pipeline step power; 

Reproducing the Analysis, section 8.21)

Outputs: stats_results/<tag>/power/power_lab.csv, power_online.csv (power of every measure, test, design and shrinkage factor), power_generating_slopes.csv (intercepts, slopes and residual SD per condition)

